Catch-all statements are used in Dynamic Routes (DRs) to match routes without specific parameters. Here's a step-by-step guide on how to use catch-all statements in DRs:

1. Determine the base route(s) for which you want to match catch-all segments. For example, if you have a base route of "/app/," and you want to match catch-all segments that start with "a" (e.g., "/app/a"), you can use the following catch-all syntax in your DRs:

```
[route, params, ...] = {
  'base': '/app',
  'parameters': [
    { 'slug': 'a' }   # Start with "a"
    { 'slug': 'b' }   # End with "b"
  ]
};
```


2. Once you have determined the base route(s) for which you want to match catch-all segments, you can use that base route(s) in your DRs by including it as a parameter in double square brackets: `[[route, params, ...] = { 'base': '/app', 'parameters': [
    { 'slug': 'a' }   # Start with "a"
    { 'slug': 'b' }   # End with "b"
  ]
};`

